Here's a clean O(N log N) solution:


cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int T;
    scanf("%d", &T);
    while (T--) {
        int N, K;
        scanf("%d %d", &N, &K);
        vector<long long> W(N);
        for (auto &w : W) scanf("%lld", &w);

        sort(W.begin(), W.end());

        // prefix sums
        vector<long long> prefix(N + 1, 0);
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + W[i];
        long long total = prefix[N];

        auto sumSmallest = [&](int cnt) { return prefix[cnt]; };
        auto sumLargest  = [&](int cnt) { return total - prefix[N - cnt]; };

        // Option 1: batch of size K = smallest K, other = largest (N-K)
        long long diff1 = sumLargest(N - K) - sumSmallest(K);

        // Option 2: batch of size K = largest K, other = smallest (N-K)
        long long diff2 = sumLargest(K) - sumSmallest(N - K);

        long long ans = max(diff1, diff2);
        printf("%lld\n", ans);
    }
    return 0;

}

Logic:

Total sum S is fixed. If one batch (size K) has sum s1, the other has S - s1, and the difference is |2·s1 − S|.
To maximize this, push s1 to an extreme: make the K-batch either the K smallest weights or the K largest weights.
Compute both resulting differences and take the max — since K ≠ N−K in general, these two extremes aren't symmetric, so both must be checked.
Using sorted array + prefix sums makes sum of smallest/largest cnt elements an O(1) lookup.

Complexity: O(N log N) per test case (dominated by sorting), well within limits for N ≤ 100, T ≤ 100.

This is the classic "K-th largest element in a stream" problem — maintain a min-heap of size K.

Approach: For each incoming score, push it into a min-heap capped at size K (pop the smallest whenever size exceeds K). Once the heap has K elements, its top (minimum of the heap) is exactly the K-th highest score seen so far. Before that, output -1.


cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int T;
    scanf("%d", &T);
    while (T--) {
        int K, N;
        scanf("%d %d", &K, &N);

        vector<long long> A(N);
        for (auto &x : A) scanf("%lld", &x);

        // min-heap holding the K largest elements seen so far
        priority_queue<long long, vector<long long>, greater<long long>> minHeap;

        vector<long long> ans(N);

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            long long val = A[i];

            if ((int)minHeap.size() < K) {
                minHeap.push(val);
            } else if (val > minHeap.top()) {
                minHeap.pop();
                minHeap.push(val);
            }
            // if val <= minHeap.top() and heap already full, it can't be in top-K, ignore

            if ((int)minHeap.size() < K) {
                ans[i] = -1;
            } else {

                ans[i] = minHeap.top();
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            printf("%lld%c", ans[i], (i + 1 == N) ? '\n' : ' ');
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

Why this works:

The min-heap always holds (up to) the K largest elements seen so far.
Its smallest element (the top) is therefore the K-th largest overall.
Every new value either grows the heap (while size < K) or, if it beats the current K-th largest, replaces it (pop smallest, push new). Otherwise it's correctly discarded since it can't be in the top K.

Complexity: O(N log K) per test stream, O(1) extra query time per event — efficient even for large N.

One note: I couldn't fully confirm the constraints section (N, K, A_i bounds) since it may be below the photographed area — I used long long to be safe against large values. Let me know the actual constraints if they differ and I can tighten/adjust the solution (e.g., switch to int for speed, or handle very large N/T with faster I/O).

Found it — two bugs in that last loop:


cpp
for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
    cout<<ans[i];
    (i+1==N)?'\n':' ';     // <-- broken
}

Problem 1 (compile error): N is capitalized but your variable is lowercase n. This won't compile — "N was not declared in this scope."

Problem 2 (even after fixing the case): (i+1==n)?'\n':' '; on its own is just a dangling expression — it evaluates the ternary but never sends it anywhere. It needs to be fed into cout, otherwise nothing is printed and all your numbers run together with no separator.

Fix:


cpp
for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
    cout<<ans[i];
    cout<<((i+1==n)?'\n':' ');
}

or more simply:


cpp
for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
    cout<<ans[i]<<(i+1==n?'\n':' ');
}

Everything else in the code (the min-heap logic, -1 handling) looks correct — this was just the output-formatting line tripping the build.

Here's the O(N log N) solution using prefix sums + binary search + a difference array.

Key insight: Since all S_i ≥ 1, prefix sums are strictly increasing. So for a fixed sender j, the set of reachable receivers on each side (right/left) forms a contiguous range — as you move further away, interference only grows. This means:

Find the farthest reachable vehicle to the right of j via binary search on prefix sums.
Find the farthest reachable vehicle to the left of j similarly.
Each sender j then contributes "+1 incoming message" to a contiguous range of receivers — apply this with a difference array instead of looping over every receiver (which would be O(N²)).

cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main(){
    int T;
    scanf("%d", &T);
    while(T--){
        int N;
        scanf("%d", &N);
        vector<long long> S(N+1), prefix(N+1, 0);
        for(int i = 1; i <= N; i++){
            scanf("%lld", &S[i]);
            prefix[i] = prefix[i-1] + S[i];
        }

        vector<long long> diff(N+2, 0);

        for(int j = 1; j <= N; j++){
            // ---- rightward reach ----
            if(j < N){
                long long target = S[j] + prefix[j];
                // largest m in [j, N-1] with prefix[m] <= target
                int m = int(upper_bound(prefix.begin()+j, prefix.begin()+N, target) - prefix.begin()) - 1;
                int R = m + 1; // farthest reachable index to the right
                diff[j+1] += 1;
                diff[R+1] -= 1;
            }
            // ---- leftward reach ----
            if(j > 1){
                long long target = prefix[j-1] - S[j];
                // smallest p in [0, j-1] with prefix[p] >= target
                int p = int(lower_bound(prefix.begin(), prefix.begin()+j, target) - prefix.begin());

                int L = max(p, 1);
                diff[L] += 1;
                diff[j] -= 1;
            }
        }

        vector<long long> ans(N+1);
        long long cur = 0;
        for(int i = 1; i <= N; i++){
            cur += diff[i];
            ans[i] = cur;
        }

        for(int i = 1; i <= N; i++){
            printf("%lld%c", ans[i], (i==N) ? '\n' : ' ');
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

Why the binary search bounds work:

Rightward: interference from j to i (i>j) is prefix[i-1] - prefix[j]. We want the largest i with this ≤ S[j], i.e. largest m = i-1 with prefix[m] ≤ S[j] + prefix[j].
Leftward: interference from j to i (i<j) is prefix[j-1] - prefix[i]. We want the smallest i with this ≤ S[j], i.e. smallest i with prefix[i] ≥ prefix[j-1] - S[j].
Both reachable sets are contiguous because prefix sums are strictly increasing (S_i ≥ 1), so binary search is valid — no need to check every receiver individually.

Complexity: O(N log N) per test case → with ΣN ≤ 10⁶, this comfortably fits in time limits (~2×10⁷ total binary-search steps).

Edge case check: N=1 → no sender/receiver pairs possible → output is 0, handled correctly since both j<N and j>1 conditions fail.
